b}\n\\]\n\nSquaring both sides of the equation, we get:\n\n\\[\n41 + 24\\sqrt{2} = (\\sqrt{a} + \\sqrt{b})^2\n\\]\n\nExpanding the right-hand side, we have:\n\n\\[\n41 + 24\\sqrt{2} = a + b + 2\\sqrt{ab}\n\\]\n\nFor the equation to hold true, the rational parts and the irrational parts must be equal on both sides. Therefore, we equate the rational parts and the irrational parts separately:\n\n\\[\na + b = 41\n\\]\n\\[\n2\\sqrt{ab} = 24\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nFrom the second equation, we can solve for \\(\\sqrt{ab}\\):\n\n\\[\n2\\sqrt{ab} = 24\\sqrt{2} \\implies \\sqrt{ab} = 12\\sqrt{2} \\implies ab = (12\\sqrt{2})^2 = 144 \\cdot 2 = 288\n\\]\n\nNow we have a system of equations:\n\n\\[\na + b = 41\n\\]\n\\[\nab = 288\n\\]\n\nWe need to find integers \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) that satisfy these equations. We can solve this system by treating it as a quadratic equation. The quadratic equation whose roots are \\(a\\) and \\(b\\) is:\n\n\\[\nt^2 - (a+b)t + ab = 0 \\implies t^2 - 41t + 288 = 0\n\\]\n\nWe solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \\(t = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\\):\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{41 \\pm \\sqrt{41^2 - 4 \\cdot 288}}{2} = \\frac{41 \\pm \\sqrt{1681 - 1152}}{2} = \\frac{41 \\pm \\sqrt{529}}{2} = \\frac{41 \\pm 23}{2}\n\\]\n\nThis gives us two solutions:\n\n\\[\nt = \\frac{41 + 23}{2} = 32 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad t = \\frac{41 - 23}{2} = 9\n\\]\n\nThus, \\(a = 32\\) and \\(b = 9\\) (or vice versa). Therefore, we can write:\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{41 + 24\\sqrt{2}} = \\sqrt{32} + \\sqrt{9} = 4\\sqrt{2} + 3\n\\]\n\nSo the simplified form of the expression is:\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{4\\sqrt{2} + 3}\n\\]"},"grade":{"answer":"\\boxed{4\\sqrt{2} + 3}","correct":true,"format":"boxed","parseable":true},"id":"intermediate_algebra:457"}
